<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brno</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brünn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brno is the second-largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and the historic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. A Přemyslid castle stood there by the 11th century, when it became the seat of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> duchy, and the town received its privileges in 1243. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spilberk-castle">Špilberk Castle</a> later served as a notorious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> prison, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Gregor Mendel</a> carried out his experiments on heredity at the city's Augustinian abbey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brno.jpg" alt="Brno" width="320" height="573" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brno-Freiheitsplatz2.jpg: SchiDD Brno_Katedrála_sv._Petra_a_Pavla_(St.Peter_and_St.Paul_Cathedral).jpg: JRodSilva Brn... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brno_Montage_IV.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
